---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
import '../../../styles/docs-prose.css';
---

<Layout title="Export & Code Generation — Varve Docs" description="Export from Varve to SVG, React/Tailwind, Flutter, SwiftUI, and PDF with CMYK and PDF/X print profiles.">
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Tools / Export & Code Generation
      </nav>

      <h1 class="site-page-title">Export & Code Generation</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        Varve offers multiple export paths for your designs: raster and vector image formats, print-ready PDFs, and production-grade code generation for React, Tailwind, Flutter, and SwiftUI.
      </p>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Export Controls in Inspector</h2>
        <p>
          Selecting any node or frame opens the <strong>Export</strong> tab in the right inspector panel. The tab is organized for zero-clutter, rapid workflows:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Quick-Add Presets</strong> — One-click buttons to instantly arm <code>+ PNG 1x</code>, <code>+ PNG 2x</code>, or <code>+ SVG</code> configurations without opening menus.</li>
          <li><strong>Format Badges</strong> — Uppercase format labels (PNG, SVG, JPG, WEBP, PDF) with live filename preview and suffix editing.</li>
          <li><strong>Inline Preflight Diagnostics</strong> — Automatic warnings for low-effective-resolution raster assets and duplicate output path collisions right inside the inspector.</li>
          <li><strong>Multi-Selection & Empty States</strong> — Clear selection guidance when nothing is selected, and batch counter headers with direct routes to advanced export.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Batch Export Workspace (Export Dialog)</h2>
        <p>
          Press <code>Ctrl+E</code>, click <strong>File &gt; Export</strong>, or select <strong>Open export workspace</strong> from the inspector to open the redesigned two-column batch workspace:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Master-Detail Two-Column Layout</strong> — The left column manages the export queue; the right column configures output destinations, naming rules, and format-specific settings.</li>
          <li><strong>Real-Time Search & Format Filters</strong> — Search jobs by filename or toggle format filter pills (<code>All</code>, <code>PNG</code>, <code>SVG</code>, <code>PDF</code>) to focus on specific deliverables in complex documents.</li>
          <li><strong>Clickable Template Token Chips</strong> — Insert tokens like <code>+&#123;name&#125;</code>, <code>+&#123;suffix&#125;</code>, <code>+&#123;ext&#125;</code>, <code>+&#123;width&#125;</code>, and <code>+&#123;height&#125;</code> into your filename template with a single click.</li>
          <li><strong>Real-Time Path Preview</strong> — Visual file and directory tree preview shows planned filenames and folders. On desktop folder exports, the Existing files choice controls what happens when those paths are already on disk.</li>
          <li><strong>Existing Files</strong> — Choose Keep both, Replace, or Skip existing for desktop folder exports. Keep both places the entire conflicting output group in an <code>Exports N</code> subfolder so generated companion files stay together.</li>
          <li><strong>Estimated Batch Size</strong> — Live calculation in the dialog footer displays total estimated size for selected outputs before export starts.</li>
          <li><strong>Dedicated Settings Cards</strong> — Contextual options for PDF/X bleed and crop marks, raster output PPI overrides, on-device AI background removal, and timeline motion export (CSS, Lottie, video).</li>
          <li><strong>Actionable Empty State</strong> — When nothing is configured yet, the dialog explains how files join the batch and links straight to the Inspector Export tab.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Image Export</h2>

        <h3>PNG</h3>
        <p>
          Raster export with configurable scale (1x, 2x, 3x). Uses the canvas rendering pipeline to produce pixel-perfect output at any resolution. Ideal for web assets, app icons, and social media graphics.
        </p>

        <h3>SVG</h3>
        <p>
          Vector export via <code>exportDocumentToSvg()</code> and <code>exportDocumentToSvgAdvanced()</code>. The SVG output preserves:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Shape geometry (rect, circle, ellipse, line, polygon, path)</li>
          <li>Text elements with font family and styling</li>
          <li>Gradient and solid fills</li>
          <li>Stroke properties (dash, cap, join, weight)</li>
          <li>Transform matrices (position, rotation, scale)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          SVG export supports optional <code>boundsOverride</code> for focused sub-region export and multi-page document support.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>PDF Export</h2>
        <p>
          Varve's PDF export is powered by the Rust <code>varve-print</code> crate, which uses <code>lopdf</code> for direct PDF content stream generation. PDF export features:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Standard PDF</strong> — Path operators for all primitive types (rect, circle, ellipse, line, polygon, star, arrow, bezier paths)</li>
          <li><strong>PDF/X-1a</strong> — Print-ready PDF with CMYK color, crop marks, registration marks, and color bars</li>
          <li><strong>PDF/X-4</strong> — Modern PDF/X with transparency and color management support</li>
          <li><strong>Font outlining</strong> — Text converted to vector paths using ab_glyph for reliable rendering (optional)</li>
          <li><strong>ICC profile embedding</strong> — Bundled sRGB, Adobe RGB, Fogra39, GRACoL, and SWOP profiles</li>
          <li><strong>Color bars and registration marks</strong> — Print production marks with 5-crosshair registration targets</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Pattern fills in print output currently support axis-aligned grid repeats with positive
          independent gaps. Standard PDF preserves tile transparency and fill opacity; PDF/X-4
          preserves transparency; PDF/X-1a converts opaque tiles to CMYK and omits transparent
          patterns with a warning. Shifted repeats, mirror, phase, rotation, negative gaps, and
          repeats estimated above 100,000 tiles are omitted with an embedded warning. Linked
          pattern fills are raster tile images in PDF, not reusable PDF pattern objects. See the{' '}
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/patterns')}>pattern workflow guide</a> for source-tile and
          SVG limits.
        </p>
        <p>
          Presentation delivery in Design is a separate local workflow: export an ordered deck or
          selected slides as a compressed raster screen PDF or numbered PNG archive. The PDF keeps
          visual appearance but does not provide editable vectors, searchable text, or tagged
          reading order. See the <a href={sitePath('/docs/presentations')}>presentation guide</a> for
          slide order, private notes, and delivery limits.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Code Generation</h2>
        <p>
          Varve generates standalone code from your designs — no Varve runtime is required to use
          the output. Effects a target cannot represent natively are rasterized from the same
          render pipeline the canvas uses (never placeholder output); anything that had to be
          approximated appears in the export report. The full contract, per-target capability
          matrix, and verification status live in the code-export architecture document.
        </p>

        <h3>SVG, HTML, CSS</h3>
        <p>
          SVG text is placed from the same measured layout engine the canvas renders with, so
          wrapped line breaks match the editor; right-to-left runs and styled rich text carry
          through. The HTML target produces a standalone semantic page with one stylesheet — the
          same bytes the code panel's sandboxed preview renders. The CSS target emits one rule per
          layer, including native lowering for inset shadows, conic gradients, layered fills, and
          blur where the design's semantics match.
        </p>

        <h3>React + Tailwind / CSS Modules</h3>
        <p>
          React bundles carry complete literal utility classes (no runtime class assembly, so
          Tailwind's compiler sees everything), styled text runs, and pre-rendered assets for
          content the target cannot paint. The web profile is verified in a clean consumer
          project: type-checked with TypeScript and production-built through Tailwind's real CSS
          generation.
        </p>

        <h3>Flutter / SwiftUI</h3>
        <p>
          Widget output with layout stacks, gradients, shadows, image fills, and rich text. These
          targets are emitted and reviewed but have not been compiled by their native SDKs in
          Varve's verification gates — the export report labels them accordingly.
        </p>

        <h3>Bundles, assets, and regeneration</h3>
        <p>
          Multi-file code exports are self-contained: code files plus content-addressed files
          under assets/, described by a manifest of content hashes. The manifest is what lets a
          future re-export tell unchanged generated files apart from files you have edited —
          edited files become explicit conflicts, handwritten files are preserved, and nothing is
          removed without your decision.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section" id="output-accessibility">
        <h2>Accessibility of exported output</h2>
        <p>
          Export preserves the semantic intent that the target format can carry, but it does not certify the final experience. The host application, email client, document viewer, and the content you add after export all affect accessibility. Use the following review boundary before publishing.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Web code:</strong> inspect generated headings, landmarks, labels, roles, focus order, image alternatives, contrast, responsive reflow, and reduced-motion behavior in the integrated page. Run an automated scan and a keyboard plus screen-reader pass.</li>
          <li><strong>Email:</strong> resolve preflight warnings for missing image alternatives, decorative images, invalid or nested links, and compatibility fallbacks. Review the plain-text version and test the delivery clients used by your audience.</li>
          <li><strong>SVG:</strong> treat standalone artwork as visual content unless the consuming document supplies an accessible name, appropriate role, and interaction semantics where needed.</li>
          <li><strong>PDF/PDF-X:</strong> print fidelity and colour management do not imply tagged-PDF reading order. If accessibility is required, check language, tags, headings, reading order, alternate text, and outlined text with a PDF accessibility checker.</li>
          <li><strong>Raster:</strong> PNG, JPEG, WebP, AVIF, and similar files contain pixels only. Add alt text, a caption, or a nearby text alternative where the asset is used, and mark decorative use in the consuming content system.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="output-accessibility-note">
          See the public <a href={sitePath('/accessibility')}>accessibility information statement</a> for Varve's authoring-tool evidence and limitations. Generated output still requires an author or integrator review.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Spec Export</h2>
        <p>
          The Spec Panel provides developer handoff information:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Measurement readouts</strong> — Position, dimensions, rotation, corner radius for selected node</li>
          <li><strong>Style specifications</strong> — Complete fill, stroke, effect, and typography properties in a structured format</li>
          <li><strong>Diff-on-change</strong> — When you modify the design, the spec panel highlights changed values with +/- badges</li>
          <li><strong>Syntax highlighting</strong> — Code block display with PrismJS-based highlighting for 6 languages</li>
          <li><strong>File save</strong> — Save spec output as a file via the Tauri <code>save_file_bytes</code> bridge</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Inspector export tab</h2>
        <p>
          The <strong>Export</strong> tab in the Inspector handles single-object
          output without leaving the canvas. It has two sub-tabs:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Format</strong> — Quick export with an advised starting format (PNG, JPEG, WebP, SVG, or PDF), a 1x/2x/3x or custom scale for raster formats, and one primary action. On the desktop app the button reads <em>Export</em> and writes through the native save dialog; in the browser it reads <em>Download</em>. SVG output also offers a copy action that produces the exact markup the save would write.</li>
          <li><strong>Code</strong> — generated SVG, CSS, Tailwind, CSS Modules, Flutter, and SwiftUI for the selected object, with copy-to-clipboard and a change indicator when the object is edited. The code view is keyboard-scrollable.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>Export configurations</strong> are the saved outputs that run whenever the object is batch exported: enable or disable each one, edit its filename suffix, and add new ones from the quick-add buttons, the built-in preset library (including bundles such as a web asset set), or a custom format — including PDF/X print standards and code targets. Formats a deployment cannot encode (for example PDF/X in the browser) are shown disabled with the reason instead of being silently accepted.
        </p>
        <p>
          The tab exports one object at a time. When several layers are selected it names the object that will be exported and offers a route to the <strong>export workspace</strong> for the batch path, which also carries destination, naming-template, and per-run resolution options.
        </p>
        <p>
          Preflight findings for the saved configurations appear above the list
          before you export (oversized output, missing fonts, format or platform
          mismatches), and every export reports its result — including a
          cancelled save dialog — in a status line that is also announced to
          assistive technology.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Export Settings</h2>
        <p>
          Configure default export preferences in Settings:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Default format</strong> — Preferred export format (PNG, SVG, PDF)</li>
          <li><strong>Scale</strong> — Default output scale (1x, 2x, 3x)</li>
          <li><strong>ICC profile</strong> — Default color profile for PDF export</li>
          <li><strong>Bleed</strong> — Default bleed width for print documents</li>
          <li><strong>Outline text</strong> — Whether to convert text to paths by default</li>
          <li><strong>Naming template</strong> — Output file naming pattern</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <div class="docs-cta">
        <h2>Related Guides</h2>
        <p>See <a href={sitePath('/docs/file-formats')}>File Formats</a> for detailed format specifications, or <a href={sitePath('/docs/settings')}>Settings</a> for export configuration.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-page {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 0;
  }
  .docs-page h1 { font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 144; letter-spacing: -0.015em; }

  .docs-section h2 { border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border-accent); padding-bottom: var(--space-3); }

  .docs-section p { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  .docs-section code:not(.code-block) { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  .output-accessibility-note {
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--border-accent);
    background: var(--surface-muted);
  }
</style>
